WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.670
I just love this place, don’t you?

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:07.660
I know it’s kinda expensive, but everything’s
so top notch!

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.460
Hey folks. Know what you want?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.140
Yes!

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:16.040
I’d like to eat the Down Payment on a House,
with a side of flambeed Emergency Fund.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.390
Excellent choice.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:18.800
And for you, sir?

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.670
I’m pretty hungry, so I’m gonna have Our
Retirement Savings, and... what the heck,

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.220
a bottle of Our Daughter’s College Tuition.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.880
Very good.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.680
Do you think we spend too much dining out?

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:34.220
Maybe… but everyone else is doing it.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.940
Besides, what’s the alternative?

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.400
Cooking at home?

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.200
At least the breadsticks are free.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:59.080
21% of Americans have no retirement savings,
and another 10% have less than $5,000.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.400
It’s even bleaker for millennials: nearly
6 in 10 have nothing saved at all.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:08.120
Now, there are a lot of reasons for this:
the increasing cost of housing, health insurance,

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.220
and of course, student debt.

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:14.090
But for many people, those costs are relatively
fixed.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.700
If you’re looking for practical areas to
cut back, one sticks out like a fly in your

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.939
soup: DINING OUT.

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:25.480
In 2015, for the first time in history, Americans
spent more money on bars and restaurants than

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.920
on groceries, with the average household now
spending more than $3,000 annually on “food

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.840
away from home,” representing between 5
and 7 percent of their total spending.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.270
Even though restaurant prices are increasing
faster than inflation, and grocery prices

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:45.450
are holding steady--or even falling!--the
average diner is still eating out around 5

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:46.450
times a week.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.920
How much is this costing us?

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.280
Well, restaurants typically mark up their
food by around 300%, meaning the price on

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.119
the menu is about 3 times what they actually
spent on the food.

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:01.639
If that seems like a lot, remember, you’re
also paying for the restaurant’s rent, labor,

00:02:01.639 --> 00:02:02.979
equipment and overhead.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:07.770
So if you live in an area where real estate
values are increasing, that also applies to

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.600
the surface area of your local restaurant’s
tabletops.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.890
And that doesn’t even include drinks!

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:18.060
A survey by Zagat found that the average restaurant
bill per person is over $36.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.020
If you do this just 3 times a week, that’s
over $5,600 a year!

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.840
By contrast, the average meal cooked at home
with groceries from the market is about $4

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.900
per person.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.310
Now you can see why so many financial advisors
focus on dining out as a place to save money.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.600
How much money could you potentially save?

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.200
I think it’s time to….

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.720
Run the Numbers!

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:49.080
Let’s say Jake here goes out for dinner
3 times a week, spending $35 each time.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:54.130
He starts every workday with a latte and croissant
at his local coffee shop and buys lunch

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:57.960
at the local food court 4 days a week 
(Fridays are pizza day at the office).

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:04.240
In one year, he’s spent over $9,000 on commercially
prepared food and drinks--over 12% of his

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.300
yearly salary of $75,000.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.450
What if Jake could get those 3 dinners down
to just one a week?

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.940
And what if he started making coffee and breakfast
at home, and brown-bagged lunches at work?

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:19.340
Assuming that all those homemade meals would
come out to around $5 each, that would mean

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.500
a yearly savings of $4,420!

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.620
That’s enough for a Hawaiian vacation!

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.460
But wait, we’re not done!

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:32.620
What if Jake took those yearly savings and
put them into an account earning 7% compound

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:33.840
interest?

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.870
In just 10 years, he’d have over $65,000.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.400
Enough for a down payment on a house!

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.030
In 20 years, he’d have almost $200,000!

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.310
All from just eating at home more often.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:49.830
Jake’s example demonstrates that even in
an era of burdensome healthcare and student

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:53.860
loan obligations, you might have some wiggle-room
in your finances.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:58.410
Even a small cutback in dining out could allow
you to finally pay off an onerous debt or

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.500
build an emergency fund.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.060
This might be easy advice to give, but harder
to put into practice.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:09.250
Many of us dine out because we’re too busy
or tired to cook after a long day of work.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.100
(if we know how to cook at all).

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.840
And going to restaurants isn’t just about
the food.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.341
It’s a social event--a place to see your
friends, to connect with co-workers, to take

00:04:17.341 --> 00:04:18.699
the edge off a stressful day.

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:21.340
So we’re not saying “never go out to eat!”

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.400
But if you want to cut down on this expense,
you first have to know WHY you’re spending

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.060
the money in the first place.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.289
Is it a CONVENIENCE or a LUXURY?

00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:34.210
If it’s a CONVENIENCE--in other words, if
you’re eating out because you don’t have

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:38.690
time to cook at home, well, the problem might
just be a lack of preparation.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:43.310
After all, brewing a pot of coffee and toasting
a bagel can be less time-consuming than waiting

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:44.370
in a Starbucks line.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.690
And can potentially save you hundreds of
dollars.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:50.610
There are lots of resources on the web for
how to make shopping and cooking cheap and

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:55.600
simple - including this video we made about
navigating the supermarket - but if you’re

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.819
still intimidated by the prospect, you could
ease yourself into it by utilizing ingredient-or-grocery

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.860
delivery services like Blue Apron or Instacart.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:08.069
These are definitely more expensive than doing
your own shopping, but still cheaper than

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:09.000
eating out.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.319
We like to use cash at the supermarket to
stay within a budget, but if you’re trying

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.940
to incentivize yourself, you could look into
a credit card that gives rewards for grocery

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:18.780
purchases.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.260
And it’s always a good idea to keep some
convenience foods like microwavable dinners

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.370
in your freezer.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:28.100
That way, on days that you really don’t
feel like cooking, you’ll have an alternative

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.969
to stopping for take-out or ordering in.

00:05:29.969 --> 00:05:35.360
On the other hand, if eating out is a LUXURY--that
is, if you’re doing it for social or psychological

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.379
needs--there still might be ways to achieve
that with less spending.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.120
For instance, you can share an entree.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.419
Restaurant portions are typically much larger
than the average diner’s appetite, because

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:50.490
it makes it easier for businesses to justify
high prices.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:54.310
If you’re there for the service and the
atmosphere, you can get that without overeating

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.550
to the point of discomfort or leaving uneaten
food behind.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.659
If you have favorite restaurants you know
you visit often, it might be a good idea to

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.740
sign up for their email lists or check Groupon
for details.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:10.360
But beware that these “bargains” are intended
to make you visit more frequently.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.880
Same goes for “diner’s reward cards”.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.980
If you’re not careful, these strategies
might actually increase your monthly dining

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:17.840
expenses.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:22.360
Alcohol is one of the biggest markups for
restaurants, so if you like to have a drink

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.640
with dinner, you might want to find places
that allow you to BYOB.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:30.590
Similarly, going to an ice cream shop after
dinner is usually much cheaper than ordering

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.909
dessert at the table.

00:06:31.909 --> 00:06:36.249
And lastly, if you find yourself buying lunch
just as an excuse to get out of the office,

00:06:36.249 --> 00:06:38.440
no one’s forcing you to eat at your desk!

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:39.560
(Hopefully.)

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.400
Take your brown bag to a nearby park or public
space.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.920
It’s the same psychological benefit without
the cost.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.919
Dining out is a big part of our culture, and
it might be an important aspect of your social

00:06:49.919 --> 00:06:54.059
or professional life, so we’re not suggesting
you cut it out cold turkey.

00:06:54.059 --> 00:06:59.210
But you shouldn’t accept any large, regular
expense without asking yourself three fundamental

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.990
questions: How much am I spending on this?

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.460
What do I get out of it?

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:06.280
Can I get the same benefit for less money?

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.020
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.100
Do you have any tips on how to save money while dining out?

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.640
Go ahead and share them with us in the comments!

